{"topics":[{"id":"topic.tenses","title":"Verb tenses","summary":"How English verbs show time: present, past and perfect forms."},{"id":"topic.modals","title":"Modal verbs","summary":"Can, must, should, might and friends: ability, obligation, advice and likelihood."},{"id":"topic.conditionals","title":"Conditionals and wishes","summary":"If-sentences, real and imagined, and how to talk about wishes and regrets."},{"id":"topic.passive","title":"The passive and causatives","summary":"Putting the action first: passive forms, reporting passives and have something done."},{"id":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported speech","summary":"Telling people what someone else said, asked or suggested."},{"id":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Relative clauses","summary":"Adding information about a noun with who, which, that, whose and more."},{"id":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Articles and determiners","summary":"A, an, the, no article, and words like some, any, each and every."},{"id":"topic.nouns-pronouns","title":"Nouns and pronouns","summary":"Plurals, possessives, countable and uncountable nouns, and pronouns."},{"id":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Adjectives and adverbs","summary":"Describing things and actions, word order and intensifiers."},{"id":"topic.comparison","title":"Comparison","summary":"Comparatives, superlatives and other ways to compare."},{"id":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Verb patterns","summary":"Which verbs take -ing, which take to, and how objects fit in."},{"id":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Questions and negatives","summary":"Forming questions, question tags, indirect questions and negatives."},{"id":"topic.linking","title":"Linking ideas","summary":"Conjunctions, linkers and discourse markers that connect ideas."},{"id":"topic.clauses","title":"Clauses and sentence structure","summary":"Time, purpose and participle clauses, and how sentences are built."},{"id":"topic.vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary","summary":"Everyday and academic words with definitions and example sentences."},{"id":"topic.listening","title":"Listening","summary":"Dictation with real sentences, to train your ear."}],"grammarPoints":[{"id":"gp.although-however-though","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.linking","title":"Although, however and though","summary":"Learn to use 'although', 'however', and 'though' to show a contrast between two ideas in or across sentences.","explanation":"We use words like *although*, *however*, and *though* to connect two ideas that are different or surprising when put together. They help us show a contrast.\n\n*Although* and *though* are conjunctions. They connect two clauses within the same sentence. 'Though' is often used in more informal situations. You can place them at the beginning of a sentence or in the middle. When 'although' or 'though' starts a sentence, we usually use a comma after the first clause.\n\n*However* is an adverb. It connects two separate sentences or two independent clauses. When 'however' starts a new sentence, it's followed by a comma. If it connects two independent clauses, it's usually placed after a semicolon and followed by a comma.","patterns":["Although + clause, clause.","Clause, although + clause.","Clause. However, clause.","Clause; however, clause.","Though + clause, clause."],"examples":[{"text":"Although it was late, we decided to finish the project.","note":"Shows a contrast between the time and the action, 'although' starts the sentence.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She enjoyed the concert, though the tickets were expensive.","note":"Connects two contrasting ideas, 'though' is used informally in the middle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The restaurant was fully booked. However, they found us a table.","note":"Connects two contrasting sentences, 'however' starts the second sentence.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He trained for months; however, he didn't win the race.","note":"Connects two independent clauses with a semicolon before 'however' and a comma after.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They tried their best, although the task was very difficult.","note":"Connects two contrasting ideas, 'although' is in the middle of the sentence.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Though she was alone, I didn't think she was lonely.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":388708,"author":"sundown"}},{"text":"Although he is very old, he is strong.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":292152,"author":"Zifre"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"However it was raining, we still went out.","right":"Although it was raining, we still went out.","note":"'However' cannot introduce a dependent clause like 'although'."},{"wrong":"The food was delicious however it was a bit cold.","right":"The food was delicious; however, it was a bit cold.","note":"'However' needs proper punctuation (semicolon before, comma after) when connecting two clauses."},{"wrong":"She felt tired. Although she had slept well.","right":"She felt tired, although she had slept well.","note":"'Although' connects two clauses within one sentence, not two separate sentences."}],"related":["gp.and-but-or-because-so","gp.despite-and-in-spite-of","gp.concession-clauses"]},{"id":"gp.as-as-comparisons","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.comparison","title":"As ... as","summary":"Use 'as...as' to compare two things that are equal, and 'not as...as' to compare things that are unequal.","explanation":"We use the structure *as + adjective/adverb + as* to show that two things are equal or the same in some way. This comparison can be about qualities, quantities, or how actions are performed.\n\nFor example, if two cars have the same speed, you can say, \"Car A is *as fast as* Car B.\" If someone sings with the same skill as another, you can say, \"She sings *as beautifully as* her sister.\"\n\nTo show that two things are *not* equal, we use *not as + adjective/adverb + as*. This means one thing has less of a quality or performs an action less intensely than the other. For instance, \"This coffee is *not as hot as* I like it\" means the coffee is cooler than the ideal temperature.\n\nRemember to use the base form of the adjective or adverb (e.g., 'fast', 'beautifully'), not a comparative form (e.g., 'faster', 'more beautifully') within the 'as...as' structure.","patterns":["as + adjective + as","as + adverb + as","not as + adjective + as","not as + adverb + as"],"examples":[{"text":"The new laptop is as expensive as the old one.","note":"Compares the equal price of two laptops.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She can run as quickly as her brother.","note":"Compares their equal speed in running.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"This movie isn't as interesting as the last one.","note":"Shows that the movie is less interesting than another.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He doesn't speak English as fluently as his colleague.","note":"Indicates his English speaking is less fluent.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The exam was as difficult as we expected.","note":"Compares the exam's difficulty to expectations, showing they were equal.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"This desk is as good as that one.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":59864,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"This book isn't as interesting as that book.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":57045,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"My car is as fast than yours.","right":"My car is as fast as yours.","note":"Always use 'as' after the adjective/adverb in this comparison."},{"wrong":"He is tall as his father.","right":"He is as tall as his father.","note":"Don't forget the first 'as' before the adjective/adverb."},{"wrong":"The weather isn't so warm as yesterday.","right":"The weather isn't as warm as yesterday.","note":"Use 'not as...as' for negative comparisons, not 'not so...as'."}],"related":["gp.comparative-adjectives","gp.superlative-adjectives","gp.the-more-the-better"]},{"id":"gp.both-either-neither","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Both, either and neither","summary":"Learn to use 'both', 'either', and 'neither' to talk about two people or things, making choices, or saying that something is not true for two options.","explanation":"We use *both* to refer to two people or things together. It means 'the two' or 'the one and the other'. When you use 'both', the verb is always plural.\n\nWe use *either* to talk about one of two choices. It means 'one or the other'. When 'either' is the subject, the verb is usually singular. We also use 'either... or...' to connect two options.\n\nWe use *neither* to say that something is not true for two people or things. It means 'not one and not the other'. When 'neither' is the subject, the verb is usually singular. We also use 'neither... nor...' to connect two negative options.","patterns":["Both + plural noun + plural verb","Both of + pronoun/determiner + plural noun + plural verb","Either + singular noun + singular verb","Either of + pronoun/determiner + plural noun + singular verb","Neither + singular noun + singular verb","Neither of + pronoun/determiner + plural noun + singular verb","Both X and Y + plural verb","Either X or Y + verb (agrees with Y)","Neither X nor Y + verb (agrees with Y)"],"examples":[{"text":"Both sisters enjoy hiking in the mountains.","note":"Refers to two sisters, and the verb 'enjoy' is plural.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You can choose either path to reach the lake.","note":"Indicates a choice between two paths.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Neither of the restaurants serves vegetarian options.","note":"Means not one and not the other restaurant serves vegetarian food. The verb 'serves' is singular.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Both the blue shirt and the green shirt are on sale.","note":"Connects two items, meaning both are on sale.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Neither my brother nor my sister lives in this city anymore.","note":"Connects two people, meaning neither of them lives here. The verb 'lives' agrees with 'sister'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Tom grew up speaking both English and French.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":1093897,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Tom walked down the street without looking either left or right.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3410746,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Both of the students is ready for the exam.","right":"Both of the students are ready for the exam.","note":"Use a plural verb with 'both'."},{"wrong":"Neither of the options are correct.","right":"Neither of the options is correct.","note":"Use a singular verb with 'neither' when it's the subject."},{"wrong":"I don't like neither of these colors.","right":"I don't like either of these colors.","note":"Avoid double negatives; 'neither' already implies a negative."}],"related":["gp.few-little-a-few-a-little","gp.articles-in-general-statements","gp.each-every-all"]},{"id":"gp.defining-relative-clauses","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Defining relative clauses","summary":"Defining relative clauses use words like 'who', 'which', 'that', and 'where' to give essential information about a noun, telling us exactly which one you mean.","explanation":"We use *defining relative clauses* to add important information about a noun. This information tells us exactly *which* person, thing, or place we are talking about. If you remove a defining relative clause, the meaning of the sentence changes or becomes unclear.\n\nWe use *who* for people, *which* for things, and *where* for places. The word *that* can be used for both people and things. We do not use commas with defining relative clauses.\n\nFor example, in \"The student *who sits next to me* is very smart,\" the clause \"who sits next to me\" tells us exactly which student. Without it, \"The student is very smart\" is less specific.","patterns":["Noun + who + verb/subject + verb","Noun + which + verb/subject + verb","Noun + that + verb/subject + verb","Place + where + subject + verb"],"examples":[{"text":"The woman who called earlier wants to reschedule.","note":" 'who called earlier' identifies which woman.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I bought the book that you recommended.","note":" 'that you recommended' specifies which book.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"This is the park where we had our first picnic.","note":" 'where we had our first picnic' tells us which park.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She fixed the computer which was making a strange noise.","note":" 'which was making a strange noise' identifies the specific computer.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Do you know the person who designed this building?","note":" 'who designed this building' clarifies which person.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I like movies who have a lot of action.","right":"I like movies that have a lot of action.","note":"Use 'that' or 'which' for things, not 'who'."},{"wrong":"The city, where I grew up, is very small.","right":"The city where I grew up is very small.","note":"Do not use commas with defining relative clauses."},{"wrong":"He is the man works at the cafe.","right":"He is the man who works at the cafe.","note":"A relative pronoun is needed to connect the clause to the noun."}],"related":["gp.non-defining-relative-clauses","gp.advanced-relative-clauses"]},{"id":"gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Don't have to or mustn't","summary":"Learn to use 'don't have to' for no obligation and 'mustn't' for prohibition, understanding the difference between not needing to do something and not being allowed to.","explanation":"We use *don't have to* (or *doesn't have to* for third person singular) when something is not necessary or there is no obligation. It means you have a choice; you can do it if you want, but it's not required.\n\nWe use *mustn't* (or *must not*) when something is forbidden or not allowed. It's a strong prohibition, meaning you absolutely cannot do it.\n\nThe key difference is choice. With 'don't have to', you have the option to do something or not. With 'mustn't', you do not have that option; the action is prohibited.","patterns":["Subject + don't/doesn't + have to + base verb","Subject + mustn't + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"You don't have to wear a uniform at this company.","note":"It's not necessary to wear a uniform; you have a choice.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Visitors mustn't touch the artwork in the museum.","note":"Touching the artwork is forbidden.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She doesn't have to work late tonight.","note":"It's not obligatory for her to work late.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You mustn't forget your passport when you travel.","note":"Forgetting your passport is strongly advised against/prohibited by necessity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We don't have to rush, but we mustn't be late for the meeting.","note":"Rushing isn't necessary, but being late is forbidden.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You don't have to do this alone.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2208603,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"You don't have to get up so early.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":69910,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"You mustn't pay for the water, it's free.","right":"You don't have to pay for the water, it's free.","note":"It's not forbidden to pay, just not necessary."},{"wrong":"Students don't have to cheat on exams.","right":"Students mustn't cheat on exams.","note":"Cheating is forbidden, not just unnecessary."},{"wrong":"He not have to work on Sundays.","right":"He doesn't have to work on Sundays.","note":"Use 'doesn't' as the auxiliary for 'have to' with 'he/she/it'."}],"related":["gp.might-may-could-for-possibility","gp.must-and-cant-for-deduction","gp.had-better"]},{"id":"gp.few-little-a-few-a-little","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Few, little, a few, a little","summary":"Learn to use 'few', 'little', 'a few', and 'a little' to talk about small quantities of countable and uncountable nouns.","explanation":"We use *few* and *a few* with *countable nouns* (things you can count, like 'books' or 'friends'). We use *little* and *a little* with *uncountable nouns* (things you can't count individually, like 'water' or 'information').\n\nThe difference between 'few/little' and 'a few/a little' is important. *Few* and *little* mean 'not many' or 'not much' and often suggest a negative idea – almost none. For example, 'I have few friends' means I don't have many friends, and it might make me feel sad.\n\n*A few* and *a little* mean 'some' or 'a small amount' and usually have a more positive or neutral meaning. For example, 'I have a few friends' means I have some friends, which is a positive thing.","patterns":["a few + countable noun (plural)","few + countable noun (plural)","a little + uncountable noun","little + uncountable noun"],"examples":[{"text":"We have a few apples left, so we can make a pie.","note":"Uses 'a few' with a countable noun ('apples') to mean 'some', with a positive outcome.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"There were few people at the concert, which was a shame.","note":"Uses 'few' with a countable noun ('people') to mean 'not many', with a negative feeling.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Could I have a little milk in my coffee, please?","note":"Uses 'a little' with an uncountable noun ('milk') to mean 'some', a neutral request.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She had little time to prepare for the presentation.","note":"Uses 'little' with an uncountable noun ('time') to mean 'not much', implying difficulty.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Even a little effort can make a big difference.","note":"Uses 'a little' with an uncountable noun ('effort') to mean 'some', with a positive impact.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I hope to get away from Tokyo for a few days.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":258942,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"There is a little water in the glass.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":61448,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I have little books on my shelf.","right":"I have few books on my shelf.","note":"'Books' are countable, so use 'few' or 'a few'."},{"wrong":"There is few water in the bottle.","right":"There is little water in the bottle.","note":"'Water' is uncountable, so use 'little' or 'a little'."},{"wrong":"I have few money, so I can buy a coffee.","right":"I have a little money, so I can buy a coffee.","note":"If you can buy something, you have 'some' money, which is 'a little'."}],"related":["gp.both-either-neither","gp.articles-in-general-statements","gp.each-every-all"]},{"id":"gp.first-conditional","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"First conditional","summary":"The first conditional describes real possibilities in the future: if something happens, something else will happen.","explanation":"We use the *first conditional* to talk about future situations that are likely or possible. It describes a real condition and its probable result. We often use it to talk about plans, warnings, or predictions.\n\nThe structure is usually 'if' + a verb in the *present simple* in the condition clause, and 'will' + the *base form* of the verb in the result clause. For example, \"If it rains, we will stay inside.\"\n\nYou can also reverse the order of the clauses. If the 'if' clause comes first, we use a comma to separate the two clauses. If the 'will' clause comes first, we don't use a comma. For example, \"We will stay inside if it rains.\"","patterns":["If + subject + present simple, subject + will + base verb","Subject + will + base verb + if + subject + present simple"],"examples":[{"text":"If I finish work early, I will go to the gym.","note":"This shows a possible future condition and its likely result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She will call you if she needs help with the project.","note":"The 'will' clause comes first, so no comma is needed.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If they don't hurry, they will miss the bus.","note":"This is a warning about a negative future possibility.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We will have a picnic if the weather is good tomorrow.","note":"This expresses a plan that depends on a future condition.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If you study hard, you will pass the exam easily.","note":"This is a prediction based on a real possibility.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If it rains tomorrow, the baseball game will be put off.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":30590,"author":"AlanF_US"}},{"text":"If you just work hard, your grades will improve a lot.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2464834,"author":"FeuDRenais2"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If it will rain, we will cancel the game.","right":"If it rains, we will cancel the game.","note":"Do not use 'will' in the 'if' clause."},{"wrong":"If I am hungry, I eat a snack.","right":"If I am hungry, I will eat a snack.","note":"Use 'will' in the main clause for a specific future possibility."},{"wrong":"If he comes, I will going too.","right":"If he comes, I will go too.","note":"After 'will', always use the base form of the verb."}],"related":["gp.second-conditional","gp.zero-conditional","gp.its-time-and-would-rather"]},{"id":"gp.gerund-or-infinitive","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"-ing or to after verbs","summary":"Learn when to use a verb ending in -ing or 'to' + infinitive after another verb.","explanation":"Some verbs are followed by another verb ending in *-ing*. For example, you *enjoy reading* or *finish working*.\n\nOther verbs are followed by *to* and the base form of the verb (the infinitive). For instance, you *decide to go* or *plan to visit*.\n\nIt's important to learn which verbs take -ing and which take 'to' + infinitive, as there isn't a simple rule. Common verbs followed by -ing include: enjoy, finish, mind, suggest, avoid, practice. Common verbs followed by 'to' + infinitive include: decide, plan, want, hope, need, agree, promise.","patterns":["verb + verb-ing","verb + to + verb"],"examples":[{"text":"I enjoy cooking for my friends.","note":"The verb 'enjoy' is followed by -ing.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They decided to travel to Italy next summer.","note":"The verb 'decide' is followed by 'to' + infinitive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Do you mind helping me with this box?","note":"The verb 'mind' is followed by -ing.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She hopes to learn a new language soon.","note":"The verb 'hope' is followed by 'to' + infinitive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We finished eating dinner late last night.","note":"The verb 'finish' is followed by -ing.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My parents enjoy skiing every winter.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":325729,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"What do you plan to major in at college?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":537052,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I enjoy to cook.","right":"I enjoy cooking.","note":"'Enjoy' is always followed by the -ing form."},{"wrong":"They decided traveling to Italy.","right":"They decided to travel to Italy.","note":"'Decide' is always followed by 'to' + infinitive."},{"wrong":"He wants go home now.","right":"He wants to go home now.","note":"'Want' is followed by 'to' + infinitive."}],"related":["gp.phrasal-verbs-basics","gp.verb-object-infinitive","gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to"]},{"id":"gp.indirect-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Indirect questions","summary":"Learn to ask polite questions using phrases like 'Could you tell me where...' followed by statement word order.","explanation":"When we ask a direct question, we often invert the subject and verb, like 'Where *is* the bus stop?' or 'What time *does* the store open?'.\n\nHowever, when we want to be more polite, we can use an *indirect question*. We start with a polite phrase, such as 'Could you tell me...' or 'Do you know...'. After this phrase, the question changes to *statement word order* (subject + verb), and we do not use 'do', 'does', or 'did'.\n\nFor example, instead of 'Where is the bus stop?', you would say 'Could you tell me where the bus stop *is*?'. Notice that 'is' comes after 'the bus stop'. Similarly, 'What time does the store open?' becomes 'Could you tell me what time the store *opens*?'.","patterns":["Could you tell me + question word + subject + verb...?","Do you know + question word + subject + verb...?"],"examples":[{"text":"Could you tell me where the nearest cafe is?","note":"The verb 'is' comes after the subject 'the nearest cafe'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Do you know what time the train leaves?","note":"We use statement order 'the train leaves', not 'does the train leave'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Could you tell me how much this book costs?","note":"The verb 'costs' follows the subject 'this book', without 'does'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I wonder when the meeting starts.","note":"Even without 'Could you tell me', the word order is subject + verb.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Do you know why she left early?","note":"The past tense verb 'left' is used directly after the subject 'she', without 'did'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Do you know what time the shopping centre closes?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":10674279,"author":"sundown"}},{"text":"Could you tell me where the post office is?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":11602028,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Could you tell me where is the restroom?","right":"Could you tell me where the restroom is?","note":"Remember to use statement word order (subject + verb) after the question word."},{"wrong":"Do you know what time does the museum close?","right":"Do you know what time the museum closes?","note":"Do not use 'do', 'does', or 'did' in indirect questions."},{"wrong":"Could you tell me how I can get to the airport?","right":"Could you tell me how I can get to the airport?","note":"Modal verbs like 'can' also follow the subject in statement order."}],"related":["gp.question-tags","gp.subject-and-object-questions","gp.question-words"]},{"id":"gp.might-may-could-for-possibility","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Might, may and could","summary":"Use 'might', 'may', and 'could' to talk about things that are possible now or in the future, but not certain.","explanation":"We use *might*, *may*, and *could* to express that something is possible. This means there's a chance something will happen or is true, but it's not definite. These three modal verbs are often interchangeable when you're talking about general possibility.\n\nFor example, saying \"It might rain later,\" \"It may rain later,\" or \"It could rain later\" all convey the same idea: there's a possibility of rain, but it's not guaranteed.\n\nTo form negative sentences, we add 'not' after the modal verb: *might not*, *may not*, *could not*. Remember to always use the base form of the main verb after these modals.","patterns":["Subject + may/might/could + base verb","Subject + may/might/could + not + base verb","May/Might/Could + Subject + base verb?"],"examples":[{"text":"The train may be delayed due to the snow.","note":"Expresses a future possibility.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She might be at the library right now.","note":"Indicates a present possibility.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We could not finish the project on time.","note":"Shows a negative possibility in the past (could not = was not possible).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Could it be true that they're moving?","note":"Asks about the possibility of something.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I might go hiking this weekend if the weather is good.","note":"Talks about a future possibility that depends on a condition.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Judging from the look of the sky, it may rain at any moment.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":18145,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"It may sound strange, but what she said is true.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":20620,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He might to call us later.","right":"He might call us later.","note":"Do not use 'to' after modal verbs."},{"wrong":"She may arrives late.","right":"She may arrive late.","note":"Always use the base form of the verb after modal verbs."},{"wrong":"They don't could find a solution.","right":"They could not find a solution.","note":"Form negatives by adding 'not' directly after the modal verb."}],"related":["gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt","gp.must-and-cant-for-deduction","gp.had-better"]},{"id":"gp.must-and-cant-for-deduction","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Must and can't for deduction","summary":"Use 'must' and 'can't' to express strong certainty about what is or isn't true in the present moment.","explanation":"When you are very sure about something based on evidence or logic, you can use *must* to say it's true. It means you believe it's almost certainly a fact right now.\n\nFor example, if someone is yawning a lot, you might say, \"They *must* be tired.\" You're making a logical guess based on what you see.\n\nOn the other hand, if you are very sure that something is *not* true, you use *can't*. This shows you believe it's impossible or highly unlikely. For instance, if you know your friend is on vacation, you might say, \"She *can't* be at work today.\"\n\nRemember, 'must' and 'can't' here are about *deduction* – making a logical conclusion – not about obligation or ability.","patterns":["Subject + must + base verb","Subject + can't + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"The restaurant is full. The food must be delicious.","note":"Deducing the food's quality from the number of customers.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He's wearing a uniform. He must work here.","note":"Concluding his profession based on his clothes.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She just ran a marathon. She must be exhausted.","note":"Inferring her state from a recent intense activity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"That can't be true; I saw him leave an hour ago.","note":"Expressing strong disbelief based on prior knowledge.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They can't be home. Their car isn't in the driveway.","note":"Deducing their absence from a missing item.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I know this must be very difficult for you.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2640570,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"It must be very lonely here all by yourself.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3178592,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He is tired, he must sleep.","right":"He must be tired.","note":"Use 'must be' for deduction about a state, not 'must' for obligation."},{"wrong":"She must not be at the office.","right":"She can't be at the office.","note":"Use 'can't' for deduction that something is impossible or untrue, not 'must not' (which means prohibition)."},{"wrong":"It must true.","right":"It must be true.","note":"Always use the base form 'be' after 'must' when talking about a state or quality."}],"related":["gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt","gp.might-may-could-for-possibility","gp.had-better"]},{"id":"gp.passive-present-and-past","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"Passive: present and past simple","summary":"Learn to describe actions where the focus is on the receiver of the action, not the doer, using present and past simple passive forms.","explanation":"The passive voice helps us focus on the action or the person/thing receiving the action, rather than the person or thing doing it. We use it when the doer is unknown, unimportant, or obvious. For example, 'The cake was eaten' focuses on the cake, not who ate it.\n\nFor actions happening regularly or general facts, we use the *present simple passive*: *is/are + past participle*. For example, 'Coffee is grown in Brazil.' or 'These cars are made in Japan.'\n\nFor actions completed in the past, we use the *past simple passive*: *was/were + past participle*. For example, 'The bridge was built in 1900.' or 'The windows were cleaned yesterday.'\n\nIf we want to mention who did the action, we can add 'by + agent' at the end of the sentence. For example, 'The novel was written *by* a famous author.'","patterns":["Subject + is/are + past participle","Subject + was/were + past participle","Subject + is/are + past participle + by + agent","Subject + was/were + past participle + by + agent"],"examples":[{"text":"Coffee is grown in warm climates.","note":"Present simple passive for a general fact.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"These reports are submitted every Friday.","note":"Present simple passive for a routine action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The old theater was renovated last year.","note":"Past simple passive for a past event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The new park was designed by a local architect.","note":"Past simple passive with 'by + agent' to show who did the action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Many decisions are made by the committee.","note":"Present simple passive with 'by + agent' for a regular action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"French and Arabic are spoken in this country.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2261018,"author":"_undertoad"}},{"text":"Nobody could explain how the thing was made.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":44162,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"The company produce these phones.","right":"These phones are produced by the company.","note":"Use the passive voice when the focus is on the phones, not the company."},{"wrong":"The letter was write yesterday.","right":"The letter was written yesterday.","note":"Always use the past participle form of the main verb."},{"wrong":"The new building is build last year.","right":"The new building was built last year.","note":"Use 'was' for past simple passive with a singular subject and a past time reference."}],"related":["gp.causative-have-and-get","gp.passive-all-tenses","gp.reporting-passive"]},{"id":"gp.past-perfect","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Past perfect","summary":"Use the past perfect (had + past participle) to show that one past action happened before another past action.","explanation":"When we talk about two events that both happened in the past, we use the past perfect to make it clear which event happened *first*. It helps us understand the order of actions.\n\nImagine you're telling a story about something that happened yesterday. If you mention an event, and then you want to talk about something that happened *even earlier* than that event, you'll use the past perfect for the earlier action.\n\nThe past perfect is formed with *had* and the *past participle* of the main verb. It's often used with time expressions like *before*, *after*, *by the time*, or *when* to connect the two past events.","patterns":["Subject + had + past participle","Subject + had not + past participle","Had + subject + past participle?"],"examples":[{"text":"By the time we arrived, the concert had already started.","note":"The concert started first, then we arrived.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She had never visited Paris before she went last summer.","note":"Never visiting Paris happened before her trip last summer.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He felt much better after he had taken the medicine.","note":"Taking the medicine happened before he felt better.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I realized I had left my wallet at home when I got to the store.","note":"Leaving the wallet happened before realizing it at the store.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They couldn't enter the building because they hadn't brought their ID cards.","note":"Not bringing ID cards happened before they tried to enter.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Tom says he thought Mary had already done that.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6261216,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"I hurried to the station only to find that the train had already left.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":19725,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"When I got home, my brother already ate dinner.","right":"When I got home, my brother had already eaten dinner.","note":"Eating dinner happened before I got home, so use past perfect."},{"wrong":"She was tired because she didn't sleep well.","right":"She was tired because she hadn't slept well.","note":"Not sleeping well happened before she felt tired."},{"wrong":"After I finished my homework, I watched TV.","right":"After I had finished my homework, I watched TV.","note":"Finishing homework happened first, so use past perfect."}],"related":["gp.present-perfect-continuous","gp.present-perfect-for-and-since","gp.present-perfect-vs-past-simple"]},{"id":"gp.phrasal-verbs-basics","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Phrasal verbs","summary":"Learn how to use phrasal verbs, which combine a verb and a particle to create new meanings, and understand when to separate them.","explanation":"Phrasal verbs are combinations of a verb and a small word, often a preposition or an adverb, called a *particle*. Together, they create a new meaning that is often different from the individual words. For example, \"look\" means to see, but \"look *for*\" means to search.\n\nSome phrasal verbs are *separable*. This means you can put the object of the verb either between the verb and the particle, or after the particle. If the object is a pronoun (like \"it,\" \"them,\" \"him\"), it *must* go between the verb and the particle. For example, \"turn on the light\" or \"turn the light on,\" but only \"turn *it* on.\"\n\nOther phrasal verbs are *inseparable*. With these, the verb and the particle always stay together. The object must always come *after* the particle. For example, \"look *after* the children\" is correct, but \"look the children after\" is not. You also say \"look *after* them,\" not \"look them after.\"","patterns":["verb + particle + object (inseparable)","verb + particle + object (separable, with noun object)","verb + object + particle (separable, with noun object)","verb + pronoun + particle (separable, with pronoun object)"],"examples":[{"text":"Could you turn on the radio?","note":"Separable: object 'the radio' comes after the particle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Please turn it on.","note":"Separable: pronoun 'it' must go between the verb and particle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I need to look for my keys.","note":"Inseparable: 'my keys' comes after the particle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She ran into an old friend at the store.","note":"Inseparable: 'an old friend' comes after the particle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He gave back the book yesterday.","note":"Separable: object 'the book' comes after the particle.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'll call you up this evening.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":243209,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Put out the light.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":322984,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I picked up it from the floor.","right":"I picked it up from the floor.","note":"When the object is a pronoun, it must go between the verb and the particle for separable phrasal verbs."},{"wrong":"She looks the children after.","right":"She looks after the children.","note":"For inseparable phrasal verbs, the object always comes after the particle."},{"wrong":"Can you turn the light?","right":"Can you turn on the light?","note":"The particle is essential for the phrasal verb's meaning."}],"related":["gp.gerund-or-infinitive","gp.verb-object-infinitive","gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to"]},{"id":"gp.present-perfect-continuous","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Present perfect continuous","summary":"Use the present perfect continuous to talk about activities that started in the past and are still happening, or have just stopped.","explanation":"We use the *present perfect continuous* to describe an action that began in the past and is still continuing in the present. It often emphasizes the *duration* of the activity.\n\nWe also use it for actions that have just finished, but their *results or effects are visible now*. In this case, the focus is on the activity itself and its recent completion.\n\nThis tense is formed with *have/has + been + the -ing form* of the main verb. It helps us connect a past activity to the present moment.","patterns":["Subject + have/has + been + verb-ing","Subject + have/has + not + been + verb-ing","Have/Has + subject + been + verb-ing?"],"examples":[{"text":"I've been waiting for you for over an hour!","note":"Shows an action that started in the past and is still continuing.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She has been practicing the piano all morning.","note":"Emphasizes the duration of an ongoing activity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Why are your clothes wet? Have you been swimming?","note":"Asks about a recently finished action with a visible result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They haven't been feeling well lately.","note":"Describes a state or activity that started in the past and continues up to now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The children have been playing outside, so they're tired.","note":"Explains the cause of a present situation (tiredness) due to a recent activity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Tom has been acting odd lately.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3342742,"author":"Hybrid"}},{"text":"Tom has been waiting for you.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":1177519,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I am waiting for two hours.","right":"I have been waiting for two hours.","note":"Use present perfect continuous for actions continuing for a duration."},{"wrong":"She has worked here since 2010.","right":"She has been working here since 2010.","note":"Use continuous for ongoing activities, especially with 'for' or 'since'."},{"wrong":"They been studying all night.","right":"They have been studying all night.","note":"Remember to include 'have' or 'has' before 'been'."}],"related":["gp.past-perfect","gp.present-perfect-for-and-since","gp.present-perfect-vs-past-simple"]},{"id":"gp.present-perfect-for-and-since","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"For and since","summary":"Learn to use 'for' to express a duration of time and 'since' to express a starting point in time, often with present perfect tenses.","explanation":"We use *for* to talk about a period of time, answering the question \"How long?\" It tells us the duration of an action or state. For example, \"for two hours,\" \"for a week,\" or \"for many years.\"\n\nWe use *since* to talk about a specific point in time when an action or state started. It answers the question \"When did it start?\" For example, \"since Monday,\" \"since 2010,\" or \"since I was a child.\"\n\nBoth 'for' and 'since' are commonly used with the present perfect simple and present perfect continuous tenses to describe actions or states that began in the past and continue up to the present moment.","patterns":["Subject + have/has + past participle + for + duration","Subject + have/has + past participle + since + specific time","Subject + have/has + been + verb-ing + for + duration","Subject + have/has + been + verb-ing + since + specific time"],"examples":[{"text":"I have lived in this apartment for five years.","note":"Uses 'for' to show the duration of living in the apartment.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She has been studying English since last summer.","note":"Uses 'since' to show the starting point of her English studies.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They haven't visited us for a long time.","note":"Uses 'for' to indicate the duration of not visiting.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He has worked at the library since he graduated.","note":"Uses 'since' to mark the specific event when his work started.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We've been waiting for the bus for twenty minutes.","note":"Uses 'for' to state the length of time we've been waiting.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I have waited for you for a long time!","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":7797399,"author":"sharris123"}},{"text":"Tom has been missing since last Monday.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3620205,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I am living here since 2018.","right":"I have been living here since 2018.","note":"Use present perfect or present perfect continuous with 'since' for ongoing actions."},{"wrong":"She hasn't called me since three days.","right":"She hasn't called me for three days.","note":"Use 'for' to express a duration, not 'since'."},{"wrong":"They have known each other for they were children.","right":"They have known each other since they were children.","note":"Use 'since' for a specific starting point in the past."}],"related":["gp.past-perfect","gp.present-perfect-continuous","gp.present-perfect-vs-past-simple"]},{"id":"gp.present-perfect-vs-past-simple","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Present perfect or past simple","summary":"Use the past simple for finished actions in the past, and the present perfect for actions that started in the past and continue or have a result now.","explanation":"We use the *past simple* to talk about actions or events that started and finished at a specific time in the past. The time is usually mentioned or understood. For example, \"I *visited* Paris last year.\" The action of visiting is complete, and \"last year\" tells us when.\n\nWe use the *present perfect* to talk about actions that started in the past and continue up to the present moment, or actions that happened at an unspecified time in the past but have a connection or result in the present. For example, \"I *have lived* here for five years\" means I started living here five years ago and still live here now.\n\nThe main difference is whether the action or event is completely finished in the past with no connection to now, or if it has a link to the present. Look for time expressions like \"yesterday,\" \"last week,\" \"in 2010\" (past simple) versus \"ever,\" \"never,\" \"so far,\" \"already,\" \"yet,\" \"since,\" \"for\" (present perfect).","patterns":["Subject + verb-ed (or irregular past simple form)","Subject + have/has + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"I finished my homework an hour ago.","note":"The action is complete at a specific past time (\"an hour ago\").","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She has worked at this company since 2018.","note":"The action started in the past and continues up to now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They have eaten all the cookies, so there are none left.","note":"The action happened in the past, and the result (no cookies) is relevant now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We went to the beach, swam in the ocean, and built a sandcastle.","note":"A series of completed actions in the past.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He has never tried sushi before.","note":"An experience that happened (or didn't happen) at an unspecified time up to now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He has never been abroad.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":294736,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Tom baked this bread yesterday afternoon.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6244634,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I have seen that movie yesterday.","right":"I saw that movie yesterday.","note":"Use past simple with specific finished time expressions like \"yesterday.\""},{"wrong":"She lived in London for ten years, but she still lives there.","right":"She has lived in London for ten years.","note":"Use present perfect for actions that started in the past and continue to the present."},{"wrong":"He has went to the store.","right":"He has gone to the store.","note":"Remember to use the past participle form of the verb for the present perfect."}],"related":["gp.past-perfect","gp.present-perfect-continuous","gp.present-perfect-for-and-since"]},{"id":"gp.question-tags","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Question tags","summary":"Question tags are short questions at the end of a statement, used to check information or invite agreement from the listener.","explanation":"We use question tags at the end of a statement to ask for confirmation or to invite someone to agree with us. They make a conversation more friendly and engaging.\n\nTo form a question tag, we usually use an auxiliary verb (like *be*, *do*, *have*) or a modal verb (like *can*, *will*) and a pronoun. The general rule is: if the main statement is positive, the tag is negative. If the main statement is negative, the tag is positive.\n\nFor example, \"It's a beautiful day, *isn't it*?\" (positive statement, negative tag). Or, \"You don't like spicy food, *do you*?\" (negative statement, positive tag). The auxiliary verb in the tag matches the verb in the main statement.","patterns":["Statement (positive) + auxiliary/modal (negative) + pronoun?","Statement (negative) + auxiliary/modal (positive) + pronoun?","You like pizza, don't you?","She isn't coming, is she?"],"examples":[{"text":"The movie was really good, wasn't it?","note":"Checks if the listener agrees about the movie.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You haven't finished your homework yet, have you?","note":"Asks for confirmation about the homework status.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They live in a big city, don't they?","note":"Invites agreement about their living situation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He can't speak French, can he?","note":"Confirms a piece of information about his language ability.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It's quite cold today, isn't it?","note":"Checks if the listener feels the same about the weather.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"You are busy, are you?","right":"You are busy, aren't you?","note":"Use a negative tag after a positive statement."},{"wrong":"She didn't come, didn't she?","right":"She didn't come, did she?","note":"Use a positive tag after a negative statement."},{"wrong":"He likes coffee, isn't he?","right":"He likes coffee, doesn't he?","note":"Use the correct auxiliary verb ('do' for present simple verbs, 'be' for 'be' verbs)."}],"related":["gp.indirect-questions","gp.subject-and-object-questions","gp.question-words"]},{"id":"gp.reflexive-pronouns","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.nouns-pronouns","title":"Reflexive pronouns","summary":"Reflexive pronouns (myself, themselves) refer back to the subject. 'By yourself' means alone. 'Each other' shows a reciprocal action.","explanation":"Reflexive pronouns like *myself*, *yourself*, *himself*, *herself*, *itself*, *ourselves*, *yourselves*, and *themselves* are used when the subject and the object of a verb are the same person or thing. For example, if you cut your own finger, you cut *yourself*.\n\nWe also use reflexive pronouns to emphasize that someone did something without help. For instance, \"They built the shed *themselves*\" means they didn't have assistance. When we say \"by + reflexive pronoun\" (e.g., *by myself*, *by herself*), it means \"alone\" or \"without anyone else.\"\n\n*Each other* is used when two or more people or things perform the same action to one another. For example, \"My friends helped *each other* with the project\" means each friend helped the other. It's important not to confuse reflexive pronouns with *each other*.","patterns":["subject + verb + reflexive pronoun","by + reflexive pronoun","subject + verb + each other"],"examples":[{"text":"I taught myself how to bake bread.","note":"The subject 'I' and the object 'myself' are the same person.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The children enjoyed themselves at the science museum.","note":"The children were the ones who enjoyed the experience.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She prefers to study by herself in the library.","note":"'By herself' means she studies alone.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They built the entire model airplane themselves.","note":"Emphasizes that they did it without help.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My neighbors often help each other with gardening.","note":"'Each other' shows a reciprocal action between the neighbors.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I cut myself shaving.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":34805,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"The house stands by itself.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":49379,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I saw me in the reflection.","right":"I saw *myself* in the reflection.","note":"Use a reflexive pronoun when the subject and object are the same."},{"wrong":"They gave themselves presents. (meaning they exchanged gifts)","right":"They gave *each other* presents.","note":"Use 'each other' for reciprocal actions between two or more people."},{"wrong":"He lives by hisself in a small apartment.","right":"He lives by *himself* in a small apartment.","note":"'Hisself' is not a standard English word; use 'himself'."}],"related":["gp.countable-and-uncountable-nouns","gp.possessive-s","gp.collective-nouns-and-agreement"]},{"id":"gp.reported-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported questions","summary":"Learn how to report questions someone asked, using words like \"asked if\" or \"wanted to know where,\" and keeping normal sentence word order.","explanation":"When we tell someone what another person asked, we use *reported questions*. We don't use quotation marks, and the structure changes from a direct question.\n\nFor *yes/no questions* (questions that can be answered with \"yes\" or \"no\"), we use \"asked *if*\" or \"asked *whether*\". For *wh-questions* (questions starting with words like \"what,\" \"where,\" \"when,\" \"why,\" \"how\"), we use the *wh-word* itself.\n\nA key rule for reported questions is to use *normal sentence word order* (subject + verb), not question word order. Also, the verb tense often shifts back one step (e.g., present simple becomes past simple).","patterns":["subject + asked + if/whether + subject + verb (past tense)","subject + wanted to know + if/whether + subject + verb (past tense)","subject + asked + wh-word + subject + verb (past tense)","subject + wanted to know + wh-word + subject + verb (past tense)"],"examples":[{"text":"She asked if I was busy.","note":"A yes/no question uses 'if', and the word order is subject + verb.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He wanted to know where the library was.","note":"A wh-question uses the wh-word, and the word order is subject + verb.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They asked whether I had enjoyed the movie.","note":"Another option for yes/no questions is 'whether', and the tense shifts.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I asked what time the store opened.","note":"The wh-phrase 'what time' is used, and the word order is subject + verb.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My friend asked if I could help her.","note":"The modal verb 'can' changes to 'could' in reported speech.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He asked where was the station.","right":"He asked where the station was.","note":"Remember to use normal subject-verb order after the wh-word."},{"wrong":"She wanted to know you liked the food.","right":"She wanted to know if I liked the food.","note":"Use 'if' or 'whether' for reported yes/no questions."},{"wrong":"They asked do you want coffee.","right":"They asked if I wanted coffee.","note":"The auxiliary verb 'do' is not used in reported questions, and the tense shifts."}],"related":["gp.reported-statements","gp.reporting-verbs","gp.nuanced-reporting-verbs"]},{"id":"gp.reported-statements","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported statements","summary":"Learn how to report what someone said using 'said that' or 'told me that', often changing the original verb tense.","explanation":"When we want to tell someone what another person said, we use *reported speech*. This is also called indirect speech. We often change the original words slightly, especially the verb tense and pronouns.\n\nWe use *said* when we don't mention who the person spoke to. For example, \"She said that she was happy.\" If we mention the person who was spoken to, we use *told* followed by an object pronoun (like 'me', 'him', 'her', 'us', 'them') or a noun. For example, \"She told me that she was happy.\"\n\nWhen reporting statements, the verb tense usually shifts back one step into the past. For instance, present simple becomes past simple, present continuous becomes past continuous, and 'will' becomes 'would'. The word 'that' is often used after 'said' or 'told' but it is optional.","patterns":["Subject + said (that) + clause (with tense change)","Subject + told + object + (that) + clause (with tense change)"],"examples":[{"text":"She said that she liked the new movie.","note":"Reports a present simple statement ('I like the new movie') as past simple.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He told me that he was working on a new project.","note":"Reports a present continuous statement ('I am working') as past continuous, using 'told me'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They said they would arrive by noon.","note":"Reports a future statement ('We will arrive') using 'would'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My friend told us that she could help with the party.","note":"Reports a statement with 'can' ('I can help') using 'could', with 'told us'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The manager said that he had already sent the email.","note":"Reports a past simple statement ('I sent the email') as past perfect.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Tom said that he wasn't planning to do that.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6225576,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Tom and Mary didn't want to do that, but they said they had to.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6496055,"author":"CT"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He told that he was busy.","right":"He said that he was busy.","note":"Use 'said' when there is no object after the verb."},{"wrong":"She said she is tired.","right":"She said she was tired.","note":"Remember to change the tense from present to past."},{"wrong":"They told me they will come.","right":"They told me they would come.","note":"Change 'will' to 'would' in reported speech."}],"related":["gp.reported-questions","gp.reporting-verbs","gp.nuanced-reporting-verbs"]},{"id":"gp.second-conditional","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Second conditional","summary":"The second conditional describes imagined or unlikely situations in the present or future and their hypothetical results.","explanation":"We use the second conditional to talk about situations that are *unreal* or *imagined* in the present or future. These are things that are not happening now, or are very unlikely to happen.\n\nThe structure is usually an 'if' clause with a past simple verb, and a main clause with 'would' + the base form of a verb. The 'if' clause describes the imagined condition, and the main clause describes the hypothetical result.\n\nFor example, \"If I *had* a million dollars, I *would travel* the world.\" This means I don't have a million dollars, so traveling the world is just an imagined possibility. We can also use 'could' or 'might' instead of 'would' to show different levels of possibility for the result.","patterns":["If + subject + past simple, subject + would + base verb","Subject + would + base verb + if + subject + past simple","If + subject + past simple, subject + could/might + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"If I won the lottery, I would buy a big house.","note":"Describes an imagined future situation and its result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She would be happier if she had more free time.","note":"Describes an unreal present situation and its hypothetical outcome.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If we lived closer to the beach, we could go swimming every day.","note":"Uses 'could' to show a possible result of an imagined situation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"What would you do if you saw a ghost?","note":"Asks about a hypothetical situation in the future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If he didn't work so much, he might have more energy.","note":"Uses 'might' to show a less certain result of an unreal present situation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If I were you, I would paint it blue.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":31109,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"What would you do if you were in Tom's position?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3821432,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If I will have time, I would visit you.","right":"If I had time, I would visit you.","note":"Use the past simple in the 'if' clause, not 'will'."},{"wrong":"If I would be taller, I could reach that shelf.","right":"If I were taller, I could reach that shelf.","note":"Do not use 'would' in the 'if' clause. Use 'were' for 'be' in hypothetical situations for all subjects, though 'was' is also common for I/he/she/it."},{"wrong":"If she studied harder, she will pass the exam.","right":"If she studied harder, she would pass the exam.","note":"Use 'would' in the main clause for the second conditional, not 'will'."}],"related":["gp.first-conditional","gp.zero-conditional","gp.its-time-and-would-rather"]},{"id":"gp.so-and-such","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"So and such","summary":"Use \"so\" and \"such\" to emphasize adjectives or noun phrases, often with a \"that-clause\" to show a result.","explanation":"We use *so* and *such* to add emphasis to adjectives or noun phrases. They make our descriptions stronger.\n\nUse *so* before an *adjective* or *adverb*. For example, \"The coffee was *so hot*.\" or \"She sings *so beautifully*.\"\n\nUse *such* before a *noun phrase* (an adjective + a noun, or just a noun). For example, \"It was *such a hot day*.\" or \"They are *such kind people*.\" Remember to use 'a' or 'an' with singular countable nouns after 'such'.\n\nOften, we follow *so* or *such* with a *that-clause* to explain the *result* of the emphasis. For example, \"The coffee was so hot *that I couldn't drink it*.\" or \"It was such a hot day *that we went to the beach*.\" The word 'that' can sometimes be omitted in informal speech, but it's good practice to include it.","patterns":["so + adjective/adverb","such + (a/an) + adjective + noun","such + plural noun","such + uncountable noun","... so + adjective/adverb + that + clause","... such + (a/an) + adjective + noun + that + clause"],"examples":[{"text":"The book was so interesting.","note":"Uses 'so' to emphasize the adjective 'interesting'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It was such an interesting book.","note":"Uses 'such' to emphasize the noun phrase 'an interesting book'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He spoke so quietly that I couldn't hear him.","note":"Uses 'so' with an adverb and a 'that-clause' to show a result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They are such friendly neighbors that everyone loves them.","note":"Uses 'such' with a plural noun phrase and a 'that-clause' to show a result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The weather was such a mess that we stayed inside all day.","note":"Uses 'such' with an uncountable noun phrase and a 'that-clause' to show a result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I was so hungry that I ate it.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":5202404,"author":"tabular"}},{"text":"That book was so boring that I couldn't finish it.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2748842,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"It was so a good movie.","right":"It was such a good movie.","note":"Use 'such' before a noun phrase (a good movie)."},{"wrong":"The soup was such delicious.","right":"The soup was so delicious.","note":"Use 'so' before an adjective (delicious)."},{"wrong":"She ran so fast, she won the race.","right":"She ran so fast that she won the race.","note":"Include 'that' to introduce the result clause."}],"related":["gp.adverbs-of-frequency","gp.adverbs-of-manner","gp.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives"]},{"id":"gp.subject-and-object-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Subject and object questions","summary":"Learn how to ask questions about the subject (who or what performs the action) or the object (who or what receives the action) of a sentence.","explanation":"When we ask a question, sometimes we want to know *who* or *what* performed the action. This is called a *subject question*. In these questions, 'who' or 'what' is the subject of the sentence, so we don't use 'do', 'does', or 'did'. The word order is similar to a statement: Who/What + verb + object?\n\nOther times, we want to know *who* or *what* received the action. This is an *object question*. In these questions, 'who' or 'what' is the object, and we use 'do', 'does', or 'did' to form the question, just like with most other questions. The word order is: Who/What + do/does/did + subject + base verb?\n\nThe key difference is whether 'who' or 'what' is doing the action (subject question, no 'do/did') or receiving the action (object question, use 'do/did').","patterns":["Subject question: Who/What + verb (in correct tense) + (object)?","Object question: Who/What + do/does/did + subject + base verb?"],"examples":[{"text":"Who called you this morning?","note":"Subject question: 'Who' performed the action of calling.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"What made that loud noise?","note":"Subject question: 'What' performed the action of making the noise.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Who did you invite to the party?","note":"Object question: 'You' performed the action; 'who' received the invitation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"What did they eat for lunch?","note":"Object question: 'They' performed the action; 'what' was eaten.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Who wants another slice of pizza?","note":"Subject question: 'Who' performs the action of wanting.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Who did you give it to?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":16543,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Who designed that museum?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":680672,"author":"Source_VOA"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Who did open the door?","right":"Who opened the door?","note":"Do not use 'do/did' in subject questions."},{"wrong":"What you saw at the museum?","right":"What did you see at the museum?","note":"Use 'do/did' and correct word order for object questions."},{"wrong":"Who you helped with the project?","right":"Who helped you with the project?","note":"If 'who' is the subject, don't use 'do/did' and keep statement-like order."}],"related":["gp.indirect-questions","gp.question-tags","gp.question-words"]},{"id":"gp.time-clauses","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"Time clauses","summary":"Learn to use time clauses with 'when', 'while', 'as soon as', 'until', and 'before' to talk about future events, using the present simple in the time clause.","explanation":"Time clauses tell us *when* something will happen. They often start with words like *when*, *while*, *as soon as*, *until*, or *before*. The main part of the sentence usually talks about a future action.\n\nIt's important to remember that even though the action in the time clause refers to the future, we use the *present simple* tense in that clause. We do not use 'will' in the time clause itself.\n\nFor example, you say \"I'll call you *when I arrive*\" (not \"when I will arrive\"). The present simple verb 'arrive' refers to a future action.","patterns":["Future action + when/while/as soon as/until/before + present simple","When/while/as soon as/until/before + present simple, + future action"],"examples":[{"text":"I'll send you the details as soon as I finish the report.","note":"The action 'finish' happens in the future, but uses present simple after 'as soon as'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"When the movie starts, please turn off your phone.","note":"The time clause comes first, using present simple 'starts' for a future event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She won't leave until her brother arrives.","note":"'Until' shows that the action 'won't leave' continues up to the point her brother arrives.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"While you are waiting, you can read this magazine.","note":"'While' indicates an action happening at the same time as another future action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Make sure you lock the door before you go to bed.","note":"'Before' shows one future action must happen before another.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Wait until your turn comes.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":266981,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Please have a seat and wait until your name is called.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":322950,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I will call you when I will arrive.","right":"I will call you when I arrive.","note":"Do not use 'will' in the time clause."},{"wrong":"As soon as he will get home, we'll eat dinner.","right":"As soon as he gets home, we'll eat dinner.","note":"Use the present simple (gets) for third person singular subjects."},{"wrong":"She waits until the bus will come.","right":"She will wait until the bus comes.","note":"The main clause needs a future tense if the action is in the future."}],"related":["gp.imperatives","gp.participle-clauses","gp.purpose-clauses"]},{"id":"gp.used-to","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Used to","summary":"Use 'used to' to talk about past habits or states that are no longer true in the present.","explanation":"We use *used to* to describe actions or situations that happened regularly or were true in the past, but are not true or do not happen anymore. It helps us compare the past with the present.\n\nFor example, if you say \"I used to live in a small town,\" it means you lived there for some time in the past, but now you live somewhere else. If you say \"My brother used to play soccer every weekend,\" it means he played regularly in the past, but he doesn't play anymore.\n\nWe use the base form of the verb after 'used to'. For negative sentences and questions, we use 'did' or 'didn't' and change 'used to' to *use to* (without the 'd').","patterns":["Subject + used to + base verb","Subject + didn't use to + base verb","Did + subject + use to + base verb?"],"examples":[{"text":"I used to walk to school every day when I was a child.","note":"Describes a past habit that no longer happens.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"There used to be a small cafe on this corner.","note":"Describes a past state that is no longer true.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My parents didn't use to travel much before they retired.","note":"Describes a past habit that didn't happen.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Did you use to enjoy classical music?","note":"Asks about a past preference or state.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She used to have long hair, but now it's short.","note":"Describes a past state that has changed.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I used to work at a restaurant in Boston.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3148400,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Tom used to be a country music singer.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2539076,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I use to live in Paris.","right":"I used to live in Paris.","note":"Remember the 'd' in 'used to' for positive statements."},{"wrong":"Did you used to like pizza?","right":"Did you use to like pizza?","note":"In questions with 'did', use 'use to' (without 'd')."},{"wrong":"He didn't used to eat vegetables.","right":"He didn't use to eat vegetables.","note":"In negative sentences with 'didn't', use 'use to' (without 'd')."}],"related":["gp.past-perfect","gp.present-perfect-continuous","gp.present-perfect-for-and-since"]},{"id":"gp.verb-object-infinitive","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Verb + object + to","summary":"Learn how to use verbs like 'want', 'ask', and 'tell' when you want someone else to do something.","explanation":"When you want, ask, or tell another person to do an action, you use a specific structure: the verb, followed by the *object* (the person), and then *to* + the base form of the verb.\n\nThis structure helps you clearly state who you are talking to and what action you expect or desire from them. For example, if you want your friend to help you, you would say, \"I *want my friend to help* me.\"\n\nRemember that the object is always the person or people who will perform the action. The 'to' before the second verb is essential in this pattern.","patterns":["want + object + to + verb","ask + object + to + verb","tell + object + to + verb"],"examples":[{"text":"I want you to finish your report by Friday.","note":"Expresses a desire for someone else to do an action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She asked her brother to pick up some groceries.","note":"Shows a request made to another person.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The teacher told the students to open their books.","note":"Gives an instruction or command to a group.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We don't want them to leave so early.","note":"Uses the negative form to express a lack of desire.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Did you ask him to join us for dinner?","note":"Forms a question using this verb pattern.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I don't want you to change.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":1887289,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Do you want me to go?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2011541,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He wants me go to the store.","right":"He wants me *to go* to the store.","note":"Always use 'to' before the second verb in this structure."},{"wrong":"They told waiting for them.","right":"They told *us to wait* for them.","note":"Don't forget the object (the person) after 'tell' or 'ask'."},{"wrong":"I asked to help me.","right":"I asked *him to help* me.","note":"Specify who you are asking to do the action."}],"related":["gp.gerund-or-infinitive","gp.phrasal-verbs-basics","gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to"]},{"id":"gp.zero-conditional","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Zero conditional","summary":"The zero conditional describes general truths, scientific facts, and things that always happen under certain conditions.","explanation":"We use the *zero conditional* to talk about things that are always true or generally accepted facts. This includes scientific facts, general rules, and habits. The result of the condition is certain and predictable.\n\nThe structure uses the *present simple* in both parts of the sentence: the 'if' clause (the condition) and the main clause (the result). You can also use 'when' instead of 'if' in zero conditional sentences, as they both express a situation that always leads to the same outcome.\n\nThe order of the clauses can be changed. If the 'if' clause comes first, we use a comma before the main clause. If the main clause comes first, we usually don't use a comma.","patterns":["If + present simple, present simple","Present simple if + present simple","When + present simple, present simple","Present simple when + present simple"],"examples":[{"text":"If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils.","note":"This is a scientific fact that is always true.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"When I don't get enough sleep, I feel tired the next day.","note":"This describes a personal habit or a general truth for the speaker.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Plants die if they don't get water.","note":"The main clause comes first, stating a general truth.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If you press this button, the machine turns off.","note":"This explains a general instruction or how a device always works.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My cat purrs when she is happy.","note":"This describes a consistent behavior of the cat.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If it will rain, the ground gets wet.","right":"If it rains, the ground gets wet.","note":"Use the present simple in the 'if' clause, not the future simple."},{"wrong":"If you study hard, you will learn a lot.","right":"If you study hard, you learn a lot.","note":"For general truths, use the present simple in the result clause, not the future simple."},{"wrong":"When I am hungry, I eat something.","right":"When I am hungry, I eat something.","note":"Ensure correct present simple verb forms for both clauses."}],"related":["gp.first-conditional","gp.second-conditional","gp.its-time-and-would-rather"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"lesson.b1.although-however-though","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.linking","title":"Although, however and though","goal":"Learn to use 'although', 'however', and 'though' to show a contrast between two ideas in or across sentences.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.although-however-though"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.although-however-though.01","ex.b1.although-however-though.02","ex.b1.although-however-though.03","ex.b1.although-however-though.04","ex.b1.although-however-though.05","ex.b1.although-however-though.09","ex.b1.although-however-though.10","ex.b1.although-however-though.11","ex.b1.although-however-though.12","ex.b1.although-however-though.13","ex.b1.although-however-though.14","ex.b1.although-however-though.15","ex.b1.although-however-though.16","ex.b1.although-however-though.17","ex.b1.although-however-though.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.as-as-comparisons","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.comparison","title":"As ... as","goal":"Use 'as...as' to compare two things that are equal, and 'not as...as' to compare things that are unequal.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.as-as-comparisons"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.01","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.02","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.03","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.04","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.08","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.09","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.10","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.11","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.12","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.13","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.14","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.15","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.16","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.17","ex.b1.as-as-comparisons.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.both-either-neither","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Both, either and neither","goal":"Learn to use 'both', 'either', and 'neither' to talk about two people or things, making choices, or saying that something is not true for two options.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.both-either-neither"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.both-either-neither.01","ex.b1.both-either-neither.02","ex.b1.both-either-neither.03","ex.b1.both-either-neither.04","ex.b1.both-either-neither.05","ex.b1.both-either-neither.06","ex.b1.both-either-neither.08","ex.b1.both-either-neither.09","ex.b1.both-either-neither.10","ex.b1.both-either-neither.11","ex.b1.both-either-neither.12","ex.b1.both-either-neither.13","ex.b1.both-either-neither.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.defining-relative-clauses","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Defining relative clauses","goal":"Defining relative clauses use words like 'who', 'which', 'that', and 'where' to give essential information about a noun, telling us exactly which one you mean.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.defining-relative-clauses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.01","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.02","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.03","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.04","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.05","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.06","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.07","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.08","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.09","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.10","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.11","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.12","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.13","ex.b1.defining-relative-clauses.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Don't have to or mustn't","goal":"Learn to use 'don't have to' for no obligation and 'mustn't' for prohibition, understanding the difference between not needing to do something and not being allowed to.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.01","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.02","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.03","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.04","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.06","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.07","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.08","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.09","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.10","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.11","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.12","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.13","ex.b1.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Few, little, a few, a little","goal":"Learn to use 'few', 'little', 'a few', and 'a little' to talk about small quantities of countable and uncountable nouns.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.few-little-a-few-a-little"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.01","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.02","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.03","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.04","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.05","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.06","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.07","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.08","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.09","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.10","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.11","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.12","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.13","ex.b1.few-little-a-few-a-little.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.first-conditional","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"First conditional","goal":"The first conditional describes real possibilities in the future: if something happens, something else will happen.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.first-conditional"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.first-conditional.01","ex.b1.first-conditional.02","ex.b1.first-conditional.03","ex.b1.first-conditional.04","ex.b1.first-conditional.05","ex.b1.first-conditional.06","ex.b1.first-conditional.07","ex.b1.first-conditional.08","ex.b1.first-conditional.09","ex.b1.first-conditional.10","ex.b1.first-conditional.12","ex.b1.first-conditional.13","ex.b1.first-conditional.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.gerund-or-infinitive","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"-ing or to after verbs","goal":"Learn when to use a verb ending in -ing or 'to' + infinitive after another verb.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.gerund-or-infinitive"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.01","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.02","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.03","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.04","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.05","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.06","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.07","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.08","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.09","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.10","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.11","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.12","ex.b1.gerund-or-infinitive.13"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.indirect-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Indirect questions","goal":"Learn to ask polite questions using phrases like 'Could you tell me where...' followed by statement word order.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.indirect-questions"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.indirect-questions.01","ex.b1.indirect-questions.02","ex.b1.indirect-questions.03","ex.b1.indirect-questions.04","ex.b1.indirect-questions.05","ex.b1.indirect-questions.06","ex.b1.indirect-questions.07","ex.b1.indirect-questions.08","ex.b1.indirect-questions.09","ex.b1.indirect-questions.10","ex.b1.indirect-questions.11","ex.b1.indirect-questions.12","ex.b1.indirect-questions.13","ex.b1.indirect-questions.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Might, may and could","goal":"Use 'might', 'may', and 'could' to talk about things that are possible now or in the future, but not certain.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.might-may-could-for-possibility"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.01","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.02","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.03","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.04","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.08","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.09","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.10","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.11","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.12","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.13","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.14","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.15","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.16","ex.b1.might-may-could-for-possibility.17"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Must and can't for deduction","goal":"Use 'must' and 'can't' to express strong certainty about what is or isn't true in the present moment.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.must-and-cant-for-deduction"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.01","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.02","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.03","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.04","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.05","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.06","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.07","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.08","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.09","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.10","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.12","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.13","ex.b1.must-and-cant-for-deduction.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.passive-present-and-past","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"Passive: present and past simple","goal":"Learn to describe actions where the focus is on the receiver of the action, not the doer, using present and past simple passive forms.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.passive-present-and-past"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.01","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.02","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.03","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.04","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.05","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.06","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.07","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.08","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.09","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.10","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.11","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.12","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.13","ex.b1.passive-present-and-past.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.past-perfect","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Past perfect","goal":"Use the past perfect (had + past participle) to show that one past action happened before another past action.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.past-perfect"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.past-perfect.01","ex.b1.past-perfect.02","ex.b1.past-perfect.03","ex.b1.past-perfect.04","ex.b1.past-perfect.05","ex.b1.past-perfect.06","ex.b1.past-perfect.07","ex.b1.past-perfect.08","ex.b1.past-perfect.09","ex.b1.past-perfect.10","ex.b1.past-perfect.11","ex.b1.past-perfect.12","ex.b1.past-perfect.13","ex.b1.past-perfect.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Phrasal verbs","goal":"Learn how to use phrasal verbs, which combine a verb and a particle to create new meanings, and understand when to separate them.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.phrasal-verbs-basics"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.01","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.02","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.03","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.04","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.05","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.06","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.07","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.10","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.11","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.12","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.13","ex.b1.phrasal-verbs-basics.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.present-perfect-continuous","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Present perfect continuous","goal":"Use the present perfect continuous to talk about activities that started in the past and are still happening, or have just stopped.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.present-perfect-continuous"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.01","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.02","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.03","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.04","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.08","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.09","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.10","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.11","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.12","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.13","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.14","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.15","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.16","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.17","ex.b1.present-perfect-continuous.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"For and since","goal":"Learn to use 'for' to express a duration of time and 'since' to express a starting point in time, often with present perfect tenses.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.present-perfect-for-and-since"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.01","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.02","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.03","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.04","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.05","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.06","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.07","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.08","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.09","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.10","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.11","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.12","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.13","ex.b1.present-perfect-for-and-since.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Present perfect or past simple","goal":"Use the past simple for finished actions in the past, and the present perfect for actions that started in the past and continue or have a result now.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.present-perfect-vs-past-simple"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.01","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.02","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.03","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.04","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.05","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.07","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.08","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.09","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.10","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.11","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.12","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.13","ex.b1.present-perfect-vs-past-simple.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.question-tags","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Question tags","goal":"Question tags are short questions at the end of a statement, used to check information or invite agreement from the listener.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.question-tags"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.question-tags.01","ex.b1.question-tags.02","ex.b1.question-tags.03","ex.b1.question-tags.04","ex.b1.question-tags.05","ex.b1.question-tags.06","ex.b1.question-tags.07","ex.b1.question-tags.08","ex.b1.question-tags.09","ex.b1.question-tags.10","ex.b1.question-tags.11","ex.b1.question-tags.12","ex.b1.question-tags.13","ex.b1.question-tags.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.reflexive-pronouns","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.nouns-pronouns","title":"Reflexive pronouns","goal":"Reflexive pronouns (myself, themselves) refer back to the subject. 'By yourself' means alone. 'Each other' shows a reciprocal action.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.reflexive-pronouns"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.01","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.02","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.03","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.04","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.05","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.07","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.08","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.10","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.11","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.12","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.13","ex.b1.reflexive-pronouns.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.reported-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported questions","goal":"Learn how to report questions someone asked, using words like \"asked if\" or \"wanted to know where,\" and keeping normal sentence word order.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.reported-questions"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.reported-questions.01","ex.b1.reported-questions.02","ex.b1.reported-questions.03","ex.b1.reported-questions.04","ex.b1.reported-questions.05","ex.b1.reported-questions.06","ex.b1.reported-questions.07","ex.b1.reported-questions.08","ex.b1.reported-questions.09","ex.b1.reported-questions.10","ex.b1.reported-questions.11","ex.b1.reported-questions.12","ex.b1.reported-questions.13","ex.b1.reported-questions.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.reported-statements","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported statements","goal":"Learn how to report what someone said using 'said that' or 'told me that', often changing the original verb tense.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.reported-statements"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.reported-statements.01","ex.b1.reported-statements.02","ex.b1.reported-statements.03","ex.b1.reported-statements.04","ex.b1.reported-statements.05","ex.b1.reported-statements.06","ex.b1.reported-statements.07","ex.b1.reported-statements.08","ex.b1.reported-statements.09","ex.b1.reported-statements.10","ex.b1.reported-statements.11","ex.b1.reported-statements.12","ex.b1.reported-statements.13","ex.b1.reported-statements.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.second-conditional","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Second conditional","goal":"The second conditional describes imagined or unlikely situations in the present or future and their hypothetical results.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.second-conditional"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.second-conditional.01","ex.b1.second-conditional.02","ex.b1.second-conditional.03","ex.b1.second-conditional.04","ex.b1.second-conditional.05","ex.b1.second-conditional.06","ex.b1.second-conditional.07","ex.b1.second-conditional.08","ex.b1.second-conditional.09","ex.b1.second-conditional.10","ex.b1.second-conditional.11","ex.b1.second-conditional.12","ex.b1.second-conditional.13","ex.b1.second-conditional.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.so-and-such","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"So and such","goal":"Use \"so\" and \"such\" to emphasize adjectives or noun phrases, often with a \"that-clause\" to show a result.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.so-and-such"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.so-and-such.01","ex.b1.so-and-such.02","ex.b1.so-and-such.03","ex.b1.so-and-such.04","ex.b1.so-and-such.05","ex.b1.so-and-such.06","ex.b1.so-and-such.07","ex.b1.so-and-such.09","ex.b1.so-and-such.10","ex.b1.so-and-such.11","ex.b1.so-and-such.12","ex.b1.so-and-such.13","ex.b1.so-and-such.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.subject-and-object-questions","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.questions-negation","title":"Subject and object questions","goal":"Learn how to ask questions about the subject (who or what performs the action) or the object (who or what receives the action) of a sentence.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.subject-and-object-questions"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.01","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.02","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.03","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.04","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.05","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.06","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.07","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.08","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.09","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.10","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.11","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.12","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.13","ex.b1.subject-and-object-questions.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.time-clauses","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"Time clauses","goal":"Learn to use time clauses with 'when', 'while', 'as soon as', 'until', and 'before' to talk about future events, using the present simple in the time clause.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.time-clauses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.time-clauses.01","ex.b1.time-clauses.02","ex.b1.time-clauses.03","ex.b1.time-clauses.04","ex.b1.time-clauses.05","ex.b1.time-clauses.06","ex.b1.time-clauses.07","ex.b1.time-clauses.08","ex.b1.time-clauses.09","ex.b1.time-clauses.10","ex.b1.time-clauses.11","ex.b1.time-clauses.12","ex.b1.time-clauses.13","ex.b1.time-clauses.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.used-to","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Used to","goal":"Use 'used to' to talk about past habits or states that are no longer true in the present.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.used-to"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b1.used-to.01","ex.b1.used-to.02","ex.b1.used-to.03","ex.b1.used-to.04","ex.b1.used-to.05","ex.b1.used-to.06","ex.b1.used-to.07","ex.b1.used-to.08","ex.b1.used-to.09","ex.b1.used-to.10","ex.b1.used-to.11","ex.b1.used-to.12","ex.b1.used-to.13","ex.b1.used-to.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b1.verb-object-infinitive","level":"B1","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Ve